      <figcaption>FIG. 3.1 · The harness around the agent. Schematic.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <ol class="parts-l" aria-label="Parts of the harness">
      <li><span class="bal" aria-hidden="true">a</span>A workflow that tracks every task</li>
      <li><span class="bal" aria-hidden="true">b</span>A learning vault</li>
      <li><span class="bal" aria-hidden="true">c</span>Project exams</li>
      <li><span class="bal" aria-hidden="true">d</span>Memory</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="prose">
      <h3><span class="bal" aria-hidden="true">a</span> A workflow that tracks every task</h3>
      <p>I call it Organic Driven Development (ODD). Every request is authorized, explored and sized before any code changes. Larger work gets a feature document in the repo, <code>odd/tasks/&lt;feature&gt;.md</code>, with numbered specs that quote my request verbatim, a task table and a log. The agent then works task by task, test-first where it applies, commits reviewable work units, and reports what was verified and what was not. Push, pull requests and publishing stay my decision. The <a href="/build-session/">build session</a> for this site shows it in practice.</p>

      <h3><span class="bal" aria-hidden="true">b</span> A learning vault</h3>
      <p>An Obsidian vault holds learning notes, kept per project. Each project runs in one of three modes: learn, log or off. Conceptual questions become concept notes. The vault never copies repo docs, so it stays a record of what I learned, not a second copy of the code.</p>

      <h3><span class="bal" aria-hidden="true">c</span> Project exams</h3>
      <p>At milestones the agent writes an open-answer exam about the project and grades my answers. The point is simple: I should be able to explain the code the agent helped write.</p>

      <h3><span class="bal" aria-hidden="true">d</span> Memory</h3>
      <p>Engram, a persistent memory, saves decisions, bug fixes and discoveries across sessions. After a break or a context compaction, the work can resume where it stopped.</p>

      <p>I wrote about the learning part in more detail: <a href={devUrl} rel="noopener">How I Built a Learning System Inside My AI Coding Workflow (Using Obsidian)</a> on DEV.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
